/-!
# Relative perfectness across a finite-order thickening of the base

Consider a cartesian diagram of schemes with `i : X ⟶ X'`, `f : X ⟶ Y`, `f' : X' ⟶ Y'` and
`j : Y ⟶ Y'`, in which `f'` is flat and locally of finite presentation and `j` is a finite-order
thickening. Stacks 0DJY asserts that an object `E'` of `D(𝒪_{X'})` whose derived pullback
`E = Li^*E'` is `Y`-perfect is itself `Y'`-perfect.

Relative perfectness (Stacks 0DI0) is the conjunction of pseudo-coherence and locally finite Tor
dimension over `f^{-1}𝒪_Y`. The first conjunct is Stacks 0DJX, which needs nothing of the square
beyond that `i` is a finite-order thickening — and it is, by Stacks 09ZU. This file carries the
*local* content of the second conjunct: the transfer of a Tor-amplitude bound, at one point of
`X`, from the local ring of `Y` to the local ring of `Y'`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Thickenings.hasTorAmplitudeCanonical_of_derivedExtensionScalars` is the
  Tor-amplitude half of Stacks 0DJG, stated with the base change presented as a pushout square of
  rings and with no finiteness hypothesis on the flat algebra. Finite presentation enters Stacks
  0DJG only through its pseudo-coherence half, and dropping it is what makes the statement usable
  at local rings.
* `Stacks.Thickenings.surjective_and_isNilpotent_ker_stalkMap`: the stalk maps of a finite-order
  thickening are surjective with nilpotent kernel.
* `Stacks.Thickenings.hasTorAmplitudeCanonical_derivedStalk_of_isPullback`: Stacks 0DJY at a
  point, for the Tor-dimension clause. At a point `x` of `X`, a Tor-amplitude bound for the stalk
  of `Li^*E'` over `𝒪_{Y, f x}` gives the same bound for the stalk of `E'` over `𝒪_{Y', j (f x)}`.
* `Stacks.Thickenings.isPseudoCoherent_of_isPullback_of_isFiniteOrderThickening`: the
  pseudo-coherence conjunct of the conclusion of Stacks 0DJY, which is where the tag sits.
* `Stacks.Thickenings.hasTorAmplitude_restrictModules_iff_forall_stalk`: Stacks 09U9 on an open
  subspace of a *bare* ringed space, with the stalks read on the ambient space. This is the rung
  that lets the open covering in part (3) of Stacks 08CG be entered at all.

## How the local statement is assembled

At a point `x` of `X` the four local rings `𝒪_{Y', j (f x)}`, `𝒪_{Y, f x}`, `𝒪_{X', i x}`,
`𝒪_{X, x}` form a pushout square. That is
`Stacks.Cohomology.StalkPushout.isPushout_stalk_of_isPullback`, and it uses that `j` is a closed
immersion: the kernel of `𝒪_{X', i x} ⟶ 𝒪_{X, x}` is generated by the image of the kernel of
`𝒪_{Y', j (f x)} ⟶ 𝒪_{Y, f x}`. Flatness of `f'` makes `𝒪_{X', i x}` a flat algebra over
`𝒪_{Y', j (f x)}`, so the two are Tor independent over it and the base-change comparison of
Stacks 065Z is invertible (Stacks 0661). The thickening makes
`𝒪_{Y', j (f x)} ⟶ 𝒪_{Y, f x}` surjective with nilpotent kernel, so the nilpotent dévissage of
Stacks 0H75 reflects the Tor-amplitude bound along it. Finally the stalk of a derived pullback is
the derived extension of scalars along the stalk map, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.derivedPullbackStalkIso`, which is what identifies the object the bound is
transferred to.

## Implementation notes

**The tag is carried only on the pseudo-coherence conjunct, and the attribute says so.** The
Tor-dimension conjunct of `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.IsPerfectRelativeTo` is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.LocallyHasFiniteTorDimension` on the inverse-image ringed space
`(X, f^{-1}𝒪_Y)`, read at a
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.CertifiedDerivedSheafTensoring`; the result proved here is
stalkwise. Reaching one from the other needs a comparison that this development does not have,
and the gap is sharp enough to name.

Unfolded, that conjunct is an open covering `{U i}` of `X` on each member of which
`HasTorAmplitude ((X, f^{-1}𝒪_Y)|_{U i})` holds for the restriction of the object. Every
mathematical input is now available:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.locallyHasFiniteTorDimension_iff_of_tensor_eq` pins the
certificate family to the canonical one;
`Stacks.Cohomology.TorAmplitude.hasTorAmplitude_of_iSup_eq_top` and
`hasTorAmplitude_restrictModules` pass between the local and the global condition at a fixed
interval;
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedTensorStalk.hasTorAmplitudeCanonical_forall_stalk_iff` is
Stacks 09U9 for an arbitrary ringed space;
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.restrictModulesStalkNatIso` of
`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Modules.StalkRestrictOpen` compares module stalks across
restriction to an open subspace of a *bare* ringed space, which is what `(X, f^{-1}𝒪_Y)` is;
`AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkFunctorRestrictScalarsFunctorIso` is the stalk of a
restriction of scalars; and `Stacks.SheafifyStalk.stalkMap_hom_eq_comp` identifies the stalk of
the structural map of `f` with the stalk map of `f` after
`Stacks.SheafifyStalk.stalkInverseImageStructureSheafIso`. It is also worth recording that
`AlgebraicGeometry.derivedStalkFunctor` is by definition
`AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkFunctor` followed by
`CategoryTheory.Functor.mapDerivedCategory`, so the vocabulary of the point-level result above
and the vocabulary of Stacks 09U9 are the same; no crossing is needed there.

The covering can now be entered:
`Stacks.Thickenings.hasTorAmplitude_restrictModules_iff_forall_stalk` above is
Stacks 09U9 on an open subspace of a bare ringed space with the stalks read on the
ambient space, which is precisely the step that part (3) of Stacks 08CG needs and that was
missing. It is built from
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedTensorStalk.hasTorAmplitudeCanonical_forall_stalk_iff`,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.restrictModulesStalkNatIso` and
`CategoryTheory.Functor.mapDerivedCategoryCompIso`, with the exactness and `CommRing` instances
of `StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Modules.StalkRestrictOpenExactness`.

What is still owed is the companion step for the *coefficients*: the stalk of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.derivedScalarRestriction` at `y`, read over the stalk of
`f^{-1}𝒪_Y`, against the stalk of the object read over `𝒪_{Y, f y}` along the stalk map of `f`.
Its two ingredients are in place —
`AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkFunctorRestrictScalarsFunctorIso` for the stalk of a
restriction of scalars, and
`Stacks.SheafifyStalk.stalkInverseImageStructureSheafIso_hom_comp_stalkMap` for the factorisation
of the resulting coefficient map through the comparison `(f^{-1}𝒪_Y)_y ≅ 𝒪_{Y, f y}` — and the
derived transport is again `CategoryTheory.Functor.mapDerivedCategoryCompIso`. The obstacle is a
third spelling of the sheaf of rings of a scheme: the target of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.inverseImageRingToStructure`, hence the right-hand side of that
first isomorphism, is `StrictlyPerfect.structureSheafOfRings X.toRingedSpace`, whereas
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.stalkMap` and `AlgebraicGeometry.derivedStalkFunctor` are read at
`X.ringCatSheaf`. The two are equal by `rfl` at default transparency but not reducibly, so
`ModuleCat ↑(TopCat.Presheaf.stalk (structureSheafOfRings X.toRingedSpace).obj y)` and
`ModuleCat ↑(X.presheaf.stalk y)` are rejected as the same type where instances are involved.
What closes it is the analogue, for `X.ringCatSheaf`, of the bridges
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.structureSheafOfRings_eq_tensorRingSheaf` and
`stalkFunctor_eq_tensorRingSheaf` already recorded for the
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf` spelling. See also the implementation notes of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Perfect.RelativelyPerfectDerivedSections`, which record that the
Tor-dimension clause of Stacks 0DI2 has no affine-locality assembly.

The point of the conclusion is written `j (f x)` and not `f' (i x)`: those are equal only
propositionally, and the structure map `𝒪_{Y', j (f x)} ⟶ 𝒪_{X', i x}` is accordingly the stalk
map of `f'` precomposed with `TopCat.Presheaf.stalkCongr`. That is the spelling the pushout
square of local rings is stated at, and the spelling at which the two scalar towers it needs can
be produced.

`Stacks.Thickenings.surjective_and_isNilpotent_ker_stalkMap` supersedes the `private`
`AlgebraicGeometry.stalkMap_ker_isNilpotent_of_finiteOrder` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Thickenings.Flatness`, which proves the same nilpotence by the
same route but cannot be used outside that file; the surjectivity clause is new. That private
copy is a dedup target: it should be deleted in favour of this one.

Both ring-level statements are in one universe. That is the generality of Stacks 065Z, whose
base-change square involves four rings and is stated with a single module universe common to all
of them, and it is the universe pattern the stalks of a `Scheme.{u}` inhabit.

